     "text": "CLINICAL SUMMARY - Payne Vega and Schwartz Medical Group\nPatient: David Chan DOB: 11/17/2005 Sex: M MRN: 283707\n\nEncounters:\n 2025-05-21 back strain BP 110/89 Wt 152\n 2024-07-30 COPD BP 120/82 Wt 147\n 2023-10-09 COPD BP 110/83 Wt 149\n 2022-12-18 URI BP 111/87 Wt 139\n 2022-02-25 COPD BP 110/80 Wt 2021-05-06 back strain BP 117/81 Wt 151\n\nAssessment/Plan:\n • COPD (FEV1 88% predicted; smoker) - controlled; meds: tiotropium 2.5 mcg\n\n---\n\nClinical Summary:\n\nDavid Chan, a 19-year-old male, has been under my care for his chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) since its onset in October 2022. His condition has remained controlled, as evidenced by his FEV1, which is currently 88% of the predicted value. Mr. Chan continues to be a smoker, despite numerous attempts to encourage cessation.\n\nOn May 21, 2025, Mr. Chan presented with a complaint of back strain. His blood pressure at that visit was 110/89 mmHg, and his weight was 152 pounds. Physical examination was otherwise unremarkable with no signs of exacerbation of his COPD. He reported no shortness of breath, no wheezing, and no chest pain.\n\nIn July 2024, Mr. Chan returned for a routine follow-up on his COPD. His blood pressure was 120/82 mmHg, and his weight was 147 pounds. Lung auscultation revealed clear breath sounds bilaterally without any adventitious sounds. Mr. Chan continues to use tiotropium 2.5 mcg with good effect. His FEV1 remained stable at 88% predicted. He reported feeling generally well, with no recent exacerbations or hospitalizations.\n\nDuring the October 2023 visit, Mr. Chan again presented with concerns related to his COPD. His blood pressure was 110/83 mmHg, and his weight was 149 pounds. Physical examination showed normal respiratory effort, with no signs of distress. The lung fields were clear, and Mr. Chan did not report any respiratory symptoms outside his usual baseline. His FEV1 was consistent at 88% of predicted values.\n\nIn December 2022, Mr. Chan visited for an upper respiratory infection (URI). His blood pressure at that time was 111/87 mmHg, and his weight was 139 pounds. Examination revealed mild congestion and postnasal drip but no signs of pneumonia or other severe respiratory issues. His COPD remained stable with no exacerbations during this visit. Mr. Chan was advised on supportive care measures for his URI, and his COPD management plan remained unchanged.\n\nThe February 2022 visit was focused on Mr. Chan's COPD management. His blood pressure was 110/80 mmHg, and his weight was 155 pounds. Pulmonary examination showed good air entry bilat"
